# Performance & FBIP { .heading-no-number }

Koka compiles to C without a garbage collector. Here is how.

---

## Perceus Reference Counting

Koka uses _Perceus_, a compile-time reference counting scheme. The compiler
inserts precise `dup`/`drop` operations so that at runtime, each value is
freed exactly when its last reference disappears. There is no tracing GC,
no pause times, and no runtime system overhead.

---

## Reuse Analysis

When a constructor is uniquely owned (reference count = 1) and the code
pattern-matches it, then rebuilds a constructor of the same size, Perceus
can _reuse_ the memory in-place. This turns functional code into
efficient imperative updates automatically.

```unchecked
fun map( xs : list<a>, f : a -> e b ) : e list<b>
  match xs
    Cons(x, xx) -> Cons(f(x), xx.map(f))
    Nil         -> Nil
```

When `xs` is unique, each `Cons` cell is reused in-place -- no allocation,
no deallocation. The generated code is equivalent to an imperative
in-place loop.

---

## FBIP Keywords

Annotate functions with optimization contracts:

| Keyword | Guarantee |
|---------|-----------|
| `fip` | No allocation/deallocation in the unique fast path, constant stack |
| `fip(n)` | Like `fip` but allows `n` constructor allocations |
| `fbip` | No allocation in the unique fast path (stack use allowed) |
| `fbip(n)` | Like `fbip` but `n` allocations allowed |
| `tail` | Constant stack space (tail-call guarantee) |

The compiler verifies these contracts -- if the code doesn't satisfy
them, you get a compile error.

---

## Example: In-Place List Reversal

```koka
fip fun reverse-acc( xs : list<a>, acc : list<a> ) : list<a>
  match xs
    Cons(x, xx) -> reverse-acc(xx, Cons(x, acc))
    Nil         -> acc
```

When `xs` is unique, each `Cons` cell is destructured and immediately
reused for the accumulator. Zero allocations, constant stack.

---

## Example: Red-Black Tree Insertion

Red-black tree rebalancing is the canonical FBIP example. The key insight:
every branch that pattern-matches a `Node` rebuilds a `Node` of the
same size, so the compiler can reuse in-place:

```unchecked
fip(1) fun ins( t : tree, k : int, v : bool ) : tree
  match t
    Node(Red, l, kx, vx, r) | ...
    Node(Black, l, kx, vx, r) | ...
    Leaf -> Node(Red, Leaf, k, v, Leaf)   // fip(1): one new node
```

The `fip(1)` annotation confirms: at most one new `Node` is allocated
(the new leaf), and all existing nodes are reused.

---

## TRMC: Tail Recursion Modulo Cons

For functions that build a result list recursively (like `map`), Koka
applies _tail-recursion modulo cons_ (TRMC). This transforms the
recursion into an in-place loop that fills in the tail pointer of each
`Cons` cell as it goes, avoiding stack growth.

